      "text": "```\nHARDWOOD vs. SOFTWOOD (NOT about actual hardness)\n==================================================="
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#030",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:woodworking:00-overview, git-history:woodworking:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "woodworking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "woodworking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:woodworking:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "woodworking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "woodworking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Woodworking: Wood, Tools, Joinery, and Finishing - Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "HARDWOOD                            SOFTWOOD\n(flowering trees — angiosperms)     (conifers — gymnosperms)\n--------------------------------    -------------------------\nVessels visible in cross-section    Tracheids only (no vessels)\nMore complex anatomy                Simpler anatomy\nExamples:                           Examples:\n  Oak, maple, walnut, cherry          Pine, cedar, fir, spruce\n  mahogany, teak, ash                 hemlock, redwood, Douglas fir\nGenerally harder (but balsa is a    Generally softer (but yew and\nhardwood; pine is harder than balsa) some pines are quite hard)"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#031",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:woodworking:00-overview, git-history:woodworking:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "woodworking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "woodworking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:woodworking:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "woodworking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "woodworking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Woodworking: Wood, Tools, Joinery, and Finishing - Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "WHEN TO USE HARDWOOD:\n  - Furniture exposed to wear (tabletops, chair parts, drawers)\n  - Visible joinery (dovetails that show)\n  - Any application requiring sharpening tools (hardwood takes\n    crisp edges better)"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#032",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:woodworking:00-overview, git-history:woodworking:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "woodworking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "woodworking/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:woodworking:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "woodworking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "woodworking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Woodworking: Wood, Tools, Joinery, and Finishing - Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "WHEN TO USE SOFTWOOD:\n  - Construction lumber (studs, joists, sheeting)\n  - Shop jigs and fixtures (cost)\n  - Rustic furniture where texture acceptable\n  - Some finish carpentry (pine, cedar trim)\n  - Certain traditional applications (Shaker pieces in pine)\n```"
    },
